 <h1>EMR Enhancer — Standalone EMR Build Plan</h1>
-<p class="mu">A standalone, context-aware, trajectory-aware and pathway-aware EMR: diagnosis or context → structured clinical fields → trajectories and pathways → clinician-approved alert rules → daily bedside tracking (vitals, fever chart, insulin, intake/output) → analytics, with FHIR R4 and SNOMED/LOINC as an <b>export</b> format. Built in small chunks so each Claude chat stays short. <b>Status: chunks 1-25 and 25A-25G are built in <code>enhancer.html</code> as a single-file browser prototype (data in <code>localStorage</code>). Phase 5 has started: chunks 26-28 are built in the repo (server, database schema, REST API with tests) and chunk 29 is partly built (<code>store.js</code> written, not yet wired into the app). NEXT: 28H, then 29a-29d, then 30-41 (now in small sub-chunks) to turn the prototype into a real multi-user system: database, login and roles, audit trail, patient records, server-side AI, shared analytics, security and deployment. THEN Phase 6 (chunks 42-47): a content lifecycle (AI-drafted request, informatician review, GitHub marketplace) where marketplace content is read-only and extended only by composition. FINALLY Phase 7 (chunks 48-52): operational context components (Orders/CPOE, Pharmacy &amp; MAR, Scheduling/ADT, and decoupled Charge Capture) built on the same repeating-row engine.</b></p>
+<p class="mu">A standalone, context-aware, trajectory-aware and pathway-aware EMR: diagnosis or context → structured clinical fields → trajectories and pathways → clinician-approved alert rules → daily bedside tracking (vitals, fever chart, insulin, intake/output) → analytics, with FHIR R4 and SNOMED/LOINC as an <b>export</b> format. Built in small chunks so each Claude chat stays short. <b>Status: chunks 1-25 and 25A-25G are built in <code>enhancer.html</code> as a single-file browser prototype (data in <code>localStorage</code>). Phase 5 has started: chunks 26-28 are built in the repo (server, database schema, REST API with tests) and chunks 28H, 29a-29d, 30a and 30b are built (the app runs through <code>store.js</code>; user sets and pathways keep every version). NEXT: 30c, then 31-41 (now in small sub-chunks) to turn the prototype into a real multi-user system: database, login and roles, audit trail, patient records, server-side AI, shared analytics, security and deployment. THEN Phase 6 (chunks 42-47): a content lifecycle (AI-drafted request, informatician review, GitHub marketplace) where marketplace content is read-only and extended only by composition. FINALLY Phase 7 (chunks 48-52): operational context components (Orders/CPOE, Pharmacy &amp; MAR, Scheduling/ADT, and decoupled Charge Capture) built on the same repeating-row engine.</b></p>
 <div class="card"><b>Status note.</b> Chunks 1-25 are marked done because <code>enhancer.html</code> already contains their features (quick bar, fever chart, daily insulin grid, flowsheet, pathways, CarePlan export, rule evaluator, AI-drafted rules, CDS card export). <code>element-sets.js</code> was not part of the files checked, so confirm there which seed sets exist (for example whether an intake/output set is already there).</div>
 <div class="card warn"><b>Decision: standalone EMR, no SMART on FHIR.</b> Running as an add-on inside another EMR forces either double data entry or a fragile mapping of that EMR's data into this app's contextual model. The app's value comes from owning its data model, so it is the system of record and the SMART launch, the EMR-read mode and Send-to-EMR are removed. FHIR R4 stays as an <b>export</b> (and SNOMED/LOINC coding stays), so records can leave the system, but nothing is imported from or written to another EMR. Revisit only if a specific hospital integration is ever required; that would be a new, separate project.</div>
 <div class="card warn"><b>Decision: content is local-free, marketplace is read-only (Phase 6).</b> Clinical content (element sets, pathways, rules) follows four rules: (1) <b>local content is free</b>: a facility or clinician can create and edit its own; (2) <b>marketplace content can be fetched and used</b>; (3) <b>installed marketplace content is read-only</b> and is replaced only by a newer published version; (4) it can be <b>extended only by composition</b>, i.e. other marketplace components added next to it, never by editing it. The marketplace is a public GitHub registry (code under MIT). FHIR stays export-only: the marketplace carries content definitions, never patient data, and nothing is read from another EMR. Details in Phase 6 below.</div>
